 ## Overview
-Adaptive Bounding Box Computation computes a tight, axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) for a projected 2D Gaussian splat based on an opacity threshold, replacing the wasteful circular or square bounding boxes of original 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of excessive tile intersections and duplicated Gaussian processing by ensuring that only Gaussians with sufficient opacity and geometric extent contribute to rendering [S2]. Unlike its sibling [2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization](1198-2d-gaussian-projection-with-covariance-stabilization.md), which ensures numerical stability of the covariance matrix, this method uses the stabilized covariance to derive an opacity-aware bounding box that significantly reduces false-positive tile intersections [S2].
+Adaptive Bounding Box Computation computes a tight, axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) for a projected 2D Gaussian splat based on an opacity threshold, replacing the wasteful circular or square bounding boxes of the original 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) method [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of excessive tile intersections and duplicated Gaussian processing by filtering out Gaussians whose opacity falls below a threshold and by deriving non-square bounding boxes from the projected 2D covariance matrix [S2]. Unlike its sibling [2D Gaussian Projection with Covariance Stabilization](1198-2d-gaussian-projection-with-covariance-stabilization.md), which ensures numerical stability of the covariance matrix, this method uses the opacity and the projected covariance to derive an adaptive bounding box that significantly reduces false-positive tile intersections [S2].
 
